        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Life Insurance</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">What happens when my 20-year term life policy expires? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Once your level term ends, the policy usually rolls into an annual renewable term (ART) rate that jumps 500% to 1,000% based on your current age. Most people simply let it lapse at that point because the mortgage is paid down, the kids are independent, and retirement savings have taken over.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Are life insurance payouts taxable to my beneficiaries? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Under Internal Revenue Code Section 101(a), death benefits paid directly to named beneficiaries are free of federal income tax. The only taxable slice is any interest that builds up if the insurer holds the payout before distributing it.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question">Can I get life insurance without a medical exam? <span>+</span></button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes. Many insurers now offer accelerated underwriting, which checks your prescription history (Milliman IntelliScript), driving record, and past medical database files (MIB) electronically. Healthy applicants can often get approved for $1,000,000 to $2,000,000 in term coverage within 24 to 48 hours with no blood draw or nurse visit.
          </div>
        </div>
